/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A part has failed when it can no longer do its job safely, which can happen long before it breaks.
   - The mode fixes which strength goes on top of the factor of safety.
   - n = failure stress/allowable stress; σallow = Syt/n (ductile) or Sut/n (brittle)
   - n is the factor of safety (no unit, n > 1); Syt and Sut are the yield and ultimate tensile strengths (MPa); σallow is the allowable or permissible stress (MPa).
   - The factor grows with uncertainty.
   - A standard fixes agreed sizes, materials, tests and dimensions so that parts from different makers fit and behave alike; a code is a standard that sets design rules for safety: load cases, stress methods, minimum factors, tests and marking.
   - Codes may replace one factor of safety with partial factors: the nominal resistance R is reduced by a resistance factor φ ≤ 1 and each load effect Q is raised by a load factor γ ≥ 1, and the check becomes φR ≥ ΣγQ.
*/
