/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - σ = P/A and τ = V/A
   - σ is the direct normal stress (MPa = N/mm²); P is the axial force along the centroidal axis (N); A is the area resisting it (mm²).
   - The normal stress is uniform only away from load points, holes and grooves; near them the stress rises (stress concentration, see failure theories and fatigue design).
   - A bending moment M (N·mm) stretches the fibres on one side of a beam and shortens those on the other.
   - σb = My/I; σb,max = Mc/I = M/Z
   - y is the distance of a fibre from the neutral axis (mm); I = ∫y² dA is the second moment of area (mm⁴); c is the distance to the outermost fibre (mm); Z = I/c is the section modulus (mm³).
   - The formula assumes a straight beam of linear elastic material, small deflections and plane sections that remain plane.
   - A torque T (N·mm) twists a circular shaft.
   - τ = Tr/J; τmax = T(d/2)/J = 16T/(πd³)
   - r is the radial distance from the axis (mm), the same r over which J = ∫r² dA is taken; J is the polar second moment of area (mm⁴): πd⁴/32 for a solid shaft, π(do⁴ − di⁴)/32 for a hollow one with outer and inner diameters do and di.
*/
