/* book Worked example: Q: A 40 mm diameter solid shaft carries an axial compressive load of 30 kN, a bending moment of 600 N·m and a torque of 450 N·m. Find the direct stress, the maximum bending stress and the maximum torsional shear stress.
   1. Units: P = 30,000 N; M = 600 × 10³ N·mm; T = 450 × 10³ N·mm; d = 40 mm.
   2. Area: A = π × 40²/4 = 1256.6 mm². Direct stress: σ = 30,000/1256.6 = 23.87 MPa, compressive (−23.87 MPa).
   3. Section modulus: Z = π × 40³/32 = 6283.2 mm³. Bending stress: σb = 600,000/6283.2 = 95.49 MPa, tension on one side and compression on the other.
   4. Polar section modulus: Zp = π × 40³/16 = 12,566 mm³. Torsional shear stress: τ = 450,000/12,566 = 35.81 MPa at the surface.
   5. Signs at the surface: on the bending-compression side the normal stresses add, −23.87 − 95.49 = −119.36 MPa; on the tension side 95.49 − 23.87 = +71.62 MPa. The shear stress 35.81 MPa acts on both.
   Answer: Direct stress 23.87 MPa (compression), maximum bending stress 95.49 MPa, maximum torsional shear 35.81 MPa; the combined normal stress is −119.36 MPa on one side and +71.62 MPa on the other. */
